From 6add175157b46e1b994a45851ea63b5a7b3026b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 14sea <wanhuaning@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 17 May 2026 18:43:58 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Phase 3 NH10 (diag): add Phase D cross-address read for byte
 alignment probe
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds Phase D to mode 'P' diagnostic firmware to disambiguate the
prior "Phase C addr-0 bytes differ between bitstreams" finding.
Phase D reads 4 bytes at three addresses using NH10 auto-terminate
between reads (no RDEN_DROP pulse needed):

  D0 — addr 0x100000 (well past 368011 B ALINX bitstream;
       expected blank → 0xff per EPCS16 erased state)
  D1 — addr 0x000000 (same as Phase C; intra-boot determinism)
  D2 — addr 0x000010 (small offset; sanity that addr arithmetic
       reaches the megafunction)

Empirical findings (HW-tested 3/3 deterministic on bitstream
bc9ba066cde2aa703f453d432b311e86, capture md5 8fba57aa at
docs/captures/diag_nh10_phase_d_alignment_probe.log):

1. D0 reads 0xff 0xff 0xff 0xff (3/3) → wrapper byte alignment
   + wait_dv handshake are fundamentally OK for blank flash.
   Phase 3 READ for "slot empty" detection is reliable.

2. D1 reads `18 81 21 00` while Phase C earlier in SAME BOOT read
   `a1 00 d1 00` from the same addr 0.  Two consecutive FAST_READs
   of the same address return different bytes.  Hypothesis: the
   megafunction's internal read_add_cntr (an lpm_counter, not
   explicitly reset between operations) keeps advancing across ops
   — the second FAST_READ reads bytes 4-7 instead of 0-3.  The
   `addr` input is loaded into the megafunction's internal addr_reg
   (per epcs_ctrl.v line 612, on `(rden|wren) & not_busy`) but the
   read_add_cntr may continue from prior position.

3. D2 reads `15 80 14 3f` at addr 0x10 — reasonable bitstream
   content; offset arithmetic does reach the megafunction.

4. Cross-bitstream variance compound: 5145a6d7 first-read=
   `7d 21 0f 00`; 43f8578e first-read=`21 00 88 00`; bc9ba066
   first-read=`a1 00 d1 00`.  Even the first FAST_READ from POR
   returns different bytes per bitstream rebuild.  Combined with
   negative timing slack (-1.182 ns setup CLOCK), this points to
   placement-dependent metastability in the SHIFT_BYTES →
   am_data_valid pipeline.

5. Phase A also reverted to non-deterministic — 3 fresh boots all
   showed `A2 done i=0xf4240 final=0x01 TIMEOUT`.  The earlier
   "3/3 OK" was specifically post-NH10-flash-warm-up; cold boots
   now consistently hit the original RDSR auto-restart issue.
   Same root cause class as #4.

PHASE 3 STATUS POST-PHASE-D:
- READ for blank flash detection (slot empty check): ✅ reliable
- READ for content verification (SHA-256 over bitstream):
  ⚠️ UNRELIABLE — bytes vary per bitstream + per consecutive read
- READ termination: ✅ NH10 auto-terminate works
- RDSR (epcs_read_status): ⚠️ INTERMITTENT (cold-boot dependent)
- Workarounds for production use:
  (a) firmware pulse CTRL_RESET before each FAST_READ to reset
      read_add_cntr — untested, simplest fix candidate
  (b) wrapper auto-issue reset between FAST_READs in MMIO
  (c) SDC constraints to meet timing (deeper rework)

PHASE 4 IMPACT: ALTREMOTE_UPDATE rework (Phase 4) does not depend
on perfect READ alignment; it can proceed against the current
state.  The READ accuracy issue will need follow-up before slot
content-verification (SHA-256) is trusted.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sw/stage2_loader/main.c | 86 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 86 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sw/stage2_loader/main.c b/sw/stage2_loader/main.c
index 27304fa..54132ef 100644
--- a/sw/stage2_loader/main.c
+++ b/sw/stage2_loader/main.c
@@ -605,6 +605,92 @@ static void mode_epcs_probe(void)
     uart_puthex32(*DP_STATUS);
     uart_puts("\r\n");
 
+    /* Wait for NH10 auto-terminate to fully release busy before any
+     * follow-on FAST_READ in Phase D.  fast_read_reg can't latch the
+     * next op until busy drops. */
+    {
+        uint32_t i;
+        for (i = 0; i < 10000u; i++) {
+            if (!(*DP_STATUS & 0x1u)) break;
+        }
+        uart_puts("[P] C6 release_wait i=");
+        uart_puthex32(i);
+        uart_puts(" STATUS=");
+        uart_puthex32(*DP_STATUS);
+        uart_puts("\r\n");
+    }
+
+    /* =================================================================
+     * Phase D — Cross-address sanity for Phase C byte interpretation
+     *
+     * Reads 4 bytes at three addresses to disambiguate the "Phase C
+     * addr-0 bytes differ between bitstreams" finding (NH9 5145a6d7
+     * = 0x7d 0x21 0x0f 0x00; NH10 43f8578e = 0x21 0x00 0x88 0x00).
+     * Hypotheses:
+     *   - off-by-one in dummy-byte handling → would show in D1 vs D2
+     *   - alignment correct, addr-0 content really is what we see →
+     *     D0 (blank-flash @ 0x100000) returns 0xff = alignment OK
+     *   - cold-boot transient → D2 (addr=0 second time, same boot)
+     *     should MATCH Phase C bytes if intra-boot deterministic
+     *
+     * Three sub-reads, each: ADDR set → CTRL=FAST_READ → shift 4 bytes
+     * → CTRL=0 → wait busy=0.  No RDEN_DROP pulse; rely on NH10
+     * auto-terminate.
+     * ================================================================= */
+    uart_puts("[P] --- Phase D: cross-address sanity ---\r\n");
+
+    static const uint32_t D_ADDRS[3] = {
+        0x100000u,  /* D0 — well past 368011 B ALINX bitstream; expect 0xff */
+        0x000000u,  /* D1 — same as Phase C; intra-boot determinism check */
+        0x000010u,  /* D2 — small offset; sanity that addr arithmetic works */
+    };
+    for (uint32_t r = 0; r < 3u; r++) {
+        uint32_t a = D_ADDRS[r];
+        *DP_ADDR = a;
+        *DP_CTRL = DC_FAST_READ;
+
+        for (uint32_t byte_i = 0; byte_i < 4u; byte_i++) {
+            *DP_CTRL = DC_FAST_READ | DC_SHIFT_BYTES;
+
+            uint32_t i;
+            uint32_t s = 0;
+            for (i = 0; i < 1000000u; i++) {
+                s = *DP_STATUS;
+                if (s & 0x2u) break;
+            }
+
+            uart_puts("[P] D");
+            uart_puthex32(r);
+            uart_puts(" addr=");
+            uart_puthex32(a);
+            uart_puts(" byte=");
+            uart_puthex32(byte_i);
+            uart_puts(" i=");
+            uart_puthex32(i);
+
+            if (s & 0x2u) {
+                uint32_t b = *DP_DATAOUT;
+                uart_puts(" DATAOUT=");
+                uart_puthex32(b);
+                uart_puts("\r\n");
+            } else {
+                uart_puts(" TIMEOUT\r\n");
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+
+        /* NH10 auto-terminate: just clear CTRL, busy will drop. */
+        *DP_CTRL = 0;
+        {
+            uint32_t i;
+            for (i = 0; i < 10000u; i++) {
+                if (!(*DP_STATUS & 0x1u)) break;
+            }
+            /* No print per release wait — keep trace compact. */
+            (void)i;
+        }
+    }
+
     uart_puts("[P] diagnostic DONE\r\n");
 
     #undef DP_BASE
-- 
2.43.0

